From 355e33114cdd71820b8e0fb072213821bfbec092 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vela <vela@hermes.local>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 18:31:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(belief): stable observation identity, so repeated
 evidence revises instead of voting twice

assert_evidence had no notion of WHAT was observed: every call appended a trail
entry and added to log_odds. Asserting the same evidence twice doubled the
log-odds (0.90 -> 1.80).

Three rules for the identity, composed by the caller via observation_key():

* WITHOUT the provenance kind - the same observation does not become a new one
  by being re-classified (observed vs extracted vs told);
* WITHOUT the timestamp - a re-import, a consolidation or a corrected clock
  must not turn one observation into two;
* WITH a local key - one source can carry several independent observations, so
  the caller names the one it means; without it the source counts as one.

Belief::update_with_id replaces the weight of an existing observation and moves
the previous (weight, timestamp) into EvidenceEntry.history, so the current
state and its development stay distinguishable. Re-assessing the same
observation with a weaker provenance changes the VALUE, never the identity.

Measured (engine/tests/evidence_identity_semantics.rs, belief_lifecycle.rs):
  same id               0.9000 -> 0.9000, trail 1
  one source, two keys  trail 2, 0.9000
  provenance change     trail 1, value 0.5000 -> 0.3750, history kept
  without id            0.9000 -> 1.8000 (unchanged behaviour)

Invariant, stated precisely: log_odds == prior + sum(weights) while no ceiling
or decay applies; the ceiling clamps (6.0 vs sum 18.0), which is documented
behaviour, not a defect.

Suite: 2295 passed, 0 failed, 8 ignored (2 = known decay defects).
